From eca29d3097e35c93a33cb34bfaabc8df55de4851 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jan Forst <ensonador@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 08:25:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] LOOM-8: random. A new player: every step of the track's own
 grid (its length and its step scale) generates a note from the pattern's
 pitch-class pool, with eight randomization knobs (note %, pitch %, rhythm %,
 octave; time %, length, vel %, gate %). The steps are its active slots: tap a
 step on, and when no step is set every step plays; a step's note only marks
 it on. The pool is picked on the piano mock (hold a step, SEQ) and the keys
 dot the notes the follow mode allows (the conductor's scale, or the chord for
 harmony and ch. deg). Pitch % and rhythm % re-roll the phrase seeds when a
 generator cycle ends, so 0 % repeats exactly and 100 % changes every cycle; a
 pulse repeats the note that fired. No format change: the pool lives in a
 pseudo-lane. random.xy; t_random_player; the manual

Co-Authored-By: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>
